AB  - The number of Typhoid cases in Jamaica for the last five-year period has apparently decreased by some 18 per cent.  So far, Phage type E1 remains the commonest type while increasing numbers of type 45 are being recognised.  The figures presented for the enteric pathogens isolated from the University College Hospital patients between 1957 and 1961, generally follow the pattern of a previous islandwide survey 1952-1956.  Salmonella typhimurium still holds first place among the food poisoning group.  Shigella sonne remains the chief cause of bacillary dysentery and in the pathogenic Escherichia coli group, type 0:111 is still the most common.  The minor variations have been discussed.  These figures are by no means representative of the true incidence of enteric infections seen and treated at this hospital as they are not all fully investigated by the laboratory.  This survey merely indicates the occurrence of the various serotypes isolated at the laboratory.  The apparent variation in incidence as compared with the earlier figures for 1952-1956 have been discussed and some attempt made to assess their epidemiological significance (AU)
UR  - https://pesquisa.bvsalud.org/portal/resource/es/med-10597
